      ══ STAGE-ACTION CLASSES: TWO LEFT THIS SET ON 2026-08-10, TWO STAYED. READ BEFORE EDITING. ══

      The four Opportunity / child-object stage-action controllers all moved into the new layer-4
      capability set Opportunity_Stage_Actions_Access. Only TWO were REMOVED from here:

          REMOVED  StageAdvanceController          (was immediately below this comment)
          REMOVED  OpportunityApprovalController   (was immediately below this comment)

      Both are ACTION-ONLY and are reachable only after lwc/dealActionGuard has already called
      hasDealActionAccess() and received true, so a non-driver never invokes them. Narrowing them
      to the deal-driver population costs nobody a clean denial.

      🔴 THE OTHER TWO DELIBERATELY STAYED, AND MUST NOT BE "FINISHED OFF" BY A LATER PASS:

          KEPT  OpportunityActionPermissionController   (see below, in alphabetical position)
          KEPT  RecordStageAdvanceController            (see below, in alphabetical position)

      Both hold a permission-QUESTION method whose entire job is to answer FALSE honestly for a
      user who is NOT authorized. If class access were limited to the deal-driver personas, an
      unauthorized user's call would fail with an Apex access error instead of a clean denial -
      and lwc/dealActionGuard and lwc/recordStageGuard both push error.body.message straight into
      a toast, so the user would be shown raw platform text ("You do not have access to the Apex
      class named ..."), which ARCHITECTURE.md section 5 forbids. The gate still fails CLOSED, so
      this is a message-quality and diagnosability regression rather than a security hole - but it
      is entirely avoidable. OpportunityActionPermissionController's own class header states this
      requirement in writing.

      RecordStageAdvanceController is stronger than a preference: APEX CLASS ACCESS IS PER-CLASS,
      NOT PER-METHOD, and that single class holds both the cacheable question
      (hasStageActionAccess, called by recordStageGuard for ANY user who clicks) and the actions
      (advance / advanceTo). It is structurally un-narrowable - there is no edit to this file that
      separates them.

      Those two class grants therefore appear in BOTH this file and
      Opportunity_Stage_Actions_Access. THAT DUPLICATION IS DELIBERATE AND IS NOT THE DUPLICATION
      THE 2026-08-10 CLEANUP WAS REMOVING. LeadActionPermissionController below is the identical,
      older precedent - it sits in both this set and Lead_Stage_Actions_Access for exactly the
      same reason.

      ⚠ SEQUENCING: this removal is the destructive half of a two-deploy change and must go
      SECOND. Opportunity_Stage_Actions_Access must already be deployed, already be a member of
      DPEG_Junior_Analyst_PSG, and that group must have finished recalculating, before these two
      classes leave this file. Verify afterwards as BOTH a real deal driver (buttons still work)
      AND a non-driver (clean denial toast, NOT raw platform Apex-access text) - the non-driver
      check is the one that would catch an error in the reasoning above.
    -->
    <classAccesses>
        <apexClass>OpportunityActionPermissionController</apexClass>
        <enabled>true</enabled>
    </classAccesses>
